From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 06:38:21 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: cbm: keep the port vocabulary the buffer manager speaks

The buffer manager reaches into two headers of the datapath registry
that sits above it: one for the dequeue-resource structures and the
port-class flags it fills in, one for a bitfield overlay of the header
the packet MAC prepends to a received frame. Neither is the registry's
own interface -- the first describes what the manager returns to a
caller, the second what the hardware writes -- so keeping the manager's
half of them here lets the registry go.

Of the vocabulary, only what the manager uses is kept. The header
overlay is not kept at all: the one field read is the source port, the
high nibble of byte two, and reading that byte directly says so in one
line where the overlay took eight bitfields to arrive at the same load.

The receive path also counted frames through two helpers of the per-port
network device driver. One was a call to a generic helper and becomes
that call; the other incremented a counter in that driver's own private
statistics, which its own get_stats64 read back, and becomes the
netdev core's dropped count, which every get_stats64 folds in. Say on
the binding function that a bound device has to carry per-CPU software
statistics, which the surviving helper dereferences unchecked.

The resource structure loses its typedef and its userspace-flavoured
integer types on the way, and its one allocation becomes kmalloc_array().

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-1
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

